It is now possible to cross link C and fortran programs.  The C program must
be compiled with the "-c" option to leave it's ".o" form around.  The program
"ctofor" is then run to convert this to Fortran object module format. The 
syntax is:

	ctofor .ofile .objfile st

where .ofile is the full name [no defualt extensions] of the input UNIX
.o file, and .objfile is the full name of the file which will receive the
.obj translation. ST is optional, and if specified, has the following meaning:
	st = 0	All symbols passed through
	st = 1	All non-compiler generated symbols passed
	st = 2	No non-global symbols passed

The following rules apply to translated programs:

	.comm regions are translated to .psects in the bss segment with
the same size. A global symbol with the same name as the psect is initialized
to the first location in the psect.

	If a psect is declared both BSS and not BSS, the BSS is disabled.
Thus space may be saved by declaring common modules BSS, which they will
remain until they are initialized.

	Since the C compiler will allocate a global symbol in the data segment
if it is initialized, special cases apply to the psects that have arisen
from .comm directive translations.  If, by the end of the first linker
pass the defining symbol of the .comm has been assigned another real value,
the psect is shifted to coincide with the core region beginning at the
new value of the named global.

	This means that if a global common region is to be initialized in a
C program the place where it is initialized must be of maximal size.

	The names from UNIX .o formats are converted as follows:
Names that start with an initial "_" have the initial "_" removed.
Names that do not start with a "_" have a "." prepended.  After this
transformation has been applied the name is truncated at six characters
of significance. Upper and lower case are not differentiated.  Note:
all external symbols generated by the C compiler have the "_" prepended
in the .o file.

	A fortran program may call a C subroutine simply by writing
the name of the subroutine followed by the arguments to it. The C
subroutine will receive two integer arguments that are not significant but
must not be destroyed, followed by the number of arguments with which it
was called, followed by the addresses of the arguments. (Fortran parameters
are call by reference!)  C functions which return an integer value will
also work correctly this way.  For functions returning a real/double value
the subroutine rccall(function name, args....) must be used. The c function
named in the call must be declared EXTERNAL to fortran, and receives its
arguments from fortran in the same screwY way.

	A C program may also call a fortran subroutine.  Since the fortran
run-time environment is quite complex the following restrictions apply:

	C programs interlinked with fortran should not use the break system
call, but, if core allocation is necessary, use the fortran ICORE routine.
	The main program should be a fortran program, even if its sole
function is to call the C subroutine.  This initializes the fortran run-time
environment.

	C calls fortran cas follows:

	fcall(fortran_subroutine_name, number_of_arguemnts, address_of_arg_1, ...)
	Or, for functions, use
		ifcall -- integers
		rfcall -- reals (real*4)
		dfcall -- double precision (real*8)
	Remember that fortran arguments are by reference, and fortran
arrays are transposed.

	The C libraries have been converted, and are names

	/usr/lib/libC -- converted form of /lib/libc.a
	/usr/lib/libA -- converted form of /lib/liba.a

EXAMPLE:

The C program is:
-----------------

int common[3];

cs1(zz1, zz2, ncount, a1, a2)
int *a1, *a2;
{
	int ww;
	int fs1();

	ww = *a1 + *a2;
	printf("Answer is %d\n", ww);

	fcall(fs1, 2, a1, a2);
}

cs2(zz1, zz2, ncount, a1, a2)
int *a1, *a2;
{
	printf("Arguments are %d %d\n", *a1, *a2);
}
-----------------

The fortran program is:
-----------------
	common /common/ iz(3)
	ix = 1
	iy = 2
	call cs1(ix, iy)
	type 99
99	format('bye')
	end

	subroutine fs1(ix, iy)
	call cs2(ix, iy)
	type 99
99	format('Back in fortran')
	return
	end
---------------

The command sequence is

% fortran fortp.for
% cc -c cprog.c
% ctofor cprog.o cprog.obj
% link fortp.obj cprog.obj -f4 /usr/lib/libC /usr/lib/libA
% fortp.out
Answer is 3		--- Output produced
Arguments are 1 2
Back in fortran
Bye
